$DIR/main.p:9:2: hits is written here with no lock that guards it, while another puroutine may use it
    hits is written at main.p:9 in handle by the puroutine started by go handle
    at main.p:41 with no lock held, and another puroutine started there writes
    it at main.p:9 in handle. Two puroutines may run these at the same time, one
    of them writing, which is a data race: the other may see a value half
    written, or lose an update.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one sync.Mutex at every access, writes and reads (a sync.RWMutex's
    RLock is enough for reads):
        mu.Lock()
        hits = v
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic, such as
    atomic.Int64, and its methods at every access.
    (see "Data races" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.11)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:44:2: &counter is a view of Counter that may change while another puroutine uses it, so it cannot be passed to a new puroutine
    The argument &counter of go bump gives another puroutine &counter, a
    *Counter: after this, both puroutines can reach the same Counter. It points
    to the package variable counter, which is written at main.p:34 while
    puroutines run. A Counter is changed through a pointer at main.p:34 (its
    field n, in bump) without a lock of the same Counter held, so the compiler
    cannot tell that this one stays the same while the other puroutine uses it.
    A view may cross to another puroutine only if what it points to cannot
    change while both may use it, or every change is synchronized: a package
    variable that is not written once puroutines run, a struct whose fields are
    never changed once it is shared or are used only under a lock of the same
    struct or atomically, memory of a pluto/sync type, or the object of a
    synced[T].
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Pass a copy of the value instead of a pointer to it, or move an owner (such
    as a vec.Vec) to the puroutine so that only it has the memory.
    To share changing data between puroutines, put it in a synced[T] and hold a
    sync.Mutex in it at every access, or use a package variable guarded by a
    lock.
    (see "Data races" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.11)
    (run "pluto explain E3501" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:48:10: count is read here without holding mu, which guards it
    Every write to count that another puroutine may run at the same time holds
    mu: main.p:22 in add (the puroutine started by go add at main.p:42). So the
    compiler infers that mu guards count, and every read must hold it too: a
    read without it may run while a write is half done.
    Here the main puroutine reads it at main.p:48 in main with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold the lock while reading:
        mu.Lock()
        v := count
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data races" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.11)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
